INSERT INTO `crop` VALUES ('Apple','1. Preparation (6 months before planting): Choose a site with full sun and well-drained soil; prepare by tilling and adding organic matter.\n2. Sowing/Planting (Fall or Spring): Plant apple trees in prepared holes at the same depth they were grown in their nursery containers, spacing them about 15-20 feet apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water thoroughly during dry periods; prune annually for shape and health.\n4. Harvesting (Fall): Harvest apples when they are firm and easily detach from the tree.','Loamy','To grow apples successfully, select varieties like Red Delicious, Granny Smith, Fuji, or Gala that suit your climate and chill hour requirements. Apples thrive in temperate climates with distinct seasons and prefer well-drained, loamy soil with a pH of 6.0 to 7.0. Plant grafted trees in late fall or early spring, ensuring full sunlight and proper spacing. Maintain consistent moisture, especially when young, and apply a balanced spring fertilizer. Use organic mulch to conserve moisture and suppress weeds. Regular pruning promotes healthy growth and air circulation. Harvest apples when they reach the desired size and color, storing them in a cool, dark place for optimal longevity.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Bajra','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Prepare the land by plowing and applying organic compost.\n2. Sowing (June-July): Sow seeds at a depth of about 3-4 cm with row spacing of around 60 cm.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly but avoid waterlogging; monitor for pests and apply treatments as necessary.\n4. Harvesting (September-October): Harvest when grains are mature; use sickles to cut plants and thresh to separate grains from chaff.','Red','Selecting drought-tolerant bajra varieties like MH-154, GHB-558, and J-104 can optimize yields in Indias arid regions. Plant bajra in well-drained, sandy-loam soil during the kharif season (June-July) or adjust planting for rabi season based on local climates. Directly sow seeds at a depth of 2-3 cm with rows spaced 30-45 cm apart. Moderate water, efficient irrigation, and fertilizers rich in nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium are key for growth. Harvest mature grains with golden brown panicles and store in a cool, dry place. Market bajra grains or value-added products like bajra flour for increased profitability.','Low','Kharif'),('Banana','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Prepare fertile, well-drained soil with plenty of organic matter; select a sunny location.\n2. Sowing/Planting (Year-round): Plant banana suckers or tissue-cultured plants at a depth of about 6 inches, spacing them about 10-15 feet apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly and mulch to retain moisture; fertilize every few months with a balanced fertilizer.\n4. Harvesting (9-12 months after planting): Harvest when bananas are plump and green; cut the entire bunch from the plant.','Loamy','To grow banana trees successfully, select varieties like Cavendish, Grand Nain, or Robusta based on your climate and disease resistance. Plant in a sunny area with well-drained, loamy soil and a pH of 5.5 to 7.0. In warm climates, planting can occur year-round, while cooler regions should plant in spring. Water deeply and consistently, especially during dry spells, and use mulch to retain moisture. Fertilize regularly, focusing on potassium and micronutrients, while pruning old leaves to promote fruit development. Harvest when bananas are full-sized, and store at room temperature or refrigerate to extend shelf life. Maintain records of practices and market demand for profitability.','High','Zaid'),('Barley','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Prepare the field by plowing and adding organic matter; ensure good drainage in the soil.\n2. Sowing (November-December): Sow seeds at a depth of about 3-5 cm, maintaining a row spacing of about 15-20 cm.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Monitor moisture levels and apply irrigation during dry spells; control weeds through manual or chemical means.\n4. Harvesting (April-May): Harvest when grains are firm; use mechanical harvesters followed by threshing for efficiency.','Loamy','Select barley varieties like RD 2035, BH 902, and DWRB 91 for their adaptability to Indian climates and disease resistance. Barley grows well in loamy soil and is sown during the rabi season (October-December). Ensure consistent moisture with furrow or drip irrigation, particularly during critical growth stages, and fertilize with nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium based on soil needs. Harvest once grains mature and dry in the field before threshing. Store barley in a dry, well-ventilated area, safe from pests. Consider value-added options, like malted barley, and assess market demand or government schemes to maximize profits.','Low to Moderate','Rabi'),('Black Cardamom','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Choose a location with well-drained soil and partial shade; enrich soil with compost.\n2. Sowing/Planting (June-July): Plant seeds or rhizomes 1/4 to 1/2 inch deep, spacing them 3-5 feet apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly and ensure good drainage; mulch to retain moisture.\n4. Harvesting (3 years after planting): Harvest pods when they are mature and dry; typically done in late summer.','Loamy','To grow black cardamom (Amomum subulatum), select varieties suited to your local climate and soil conditions. This spice flourishes in warm, humid tropical climates, particularly in subtropical regions with temperatures between 20°C and 30°C (68°F to 86°F). It prefers partial shade and well-draining, fertile soil with a pH of 6.0 to 8.0, enhanced with organic matter. Propagate through seeds, spacing plants at least 3 feet apart. Maintain consistent moisture, using mulch to retain humidity and control weeds. Apply balanced fertilizer before and during the growing season. Harvest pods when brown and papery, storing them in a cool, dry place for optimal flavor.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Broccoli','1. Preparation (4-6 weeks before planting): Prepare fertile, well-drained soil enriched with compost; choose a sunny location.\n2. Sowing (Indoors or Outdoors): Sow seeds indoors or directly outdoors in early spring or late summer for fall crops.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly and weed to prevent competition; apply nitrogen fertilizer as needed.\n4. Harvesting (60-100 days after sowing): Harvest when heads are tight and green, cutting just below the head.','Loamy','To grow broccoli successfully, select varieties like Calabrese, Waltham, or De Cicco that match your climate. Broccoli thrives in cool temperatures between 45°F and 75°F, making it suitable for spring and fall planting. Use well-drained, fertile soil with a pH of 6.0 to 7.5, enriched with organic matter. Consistent moisture is vital for head formation, so water regularly and apply mulch to retain moisture. Fertilize before planting and throughout the season as needed. Harvest when heads are firm and compact, cutting with a sharp knife. Store in the refrigerator for up to a week or blanch and freeze for longer preservation.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Cabbage','1. Preparation (4-6 weeks before planting): Prepare fertile, well-drained soil enriched with compost; choose a sunny location.\n2. Sowing (Indoors or Outdoors): Sow seeds indoors or directly outdoors in early spring or late summer for fall crops.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly and weed to prevent competition; apply nitrogen fertilizer as needed.\n4. Harvesting (70-100 days after sowing): Harvest when heads are firm and fully formed, cutting at the base of the plant.','Loamy','To grow cabbage successfully, select varieties like Green, Red, or Savoy that fit your climate. Cabbage prefers cool temperatures between 45°F and 75°F and can tolerate light frosts, making it ideal for spring and fall planting. Use well-drained, fertile soil with a pH of 6.0 to 7.5, enriching it with organic matter. Water consistently to maintain moisture, and apply mulch to suppress weeds. Fertilize before planting and during the growing season, monitoring soil nutrient levels. Harvest when heads are firm, cutting just above the soil. Store in the refrigerator for a few weeks or preserve by pickling.','Moderate','Rabi'),('Carnation','1. Preparation (2-3 months before planting): Prepare rich, well-drained soil in a sunny location; amend with compost if necessary.\n2. Sowing (Indoors January-March or Outdoors April-May): Sow seeds indoors or directly outdoors depending on climate; thin seedlings as they grow.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly and provide support for taller varieties; fertilize every few weeks.\n4. Harvesting (Summer): Harvest flowers when buds are firm but not fully open for best vase life.','Loamy','Select carnation varieties like Standard, Spray, or Mini Carnations for Indian climates. Plant during the post-monsoon or early spring season in well-drained loamy soil with a pH of 6.0-7.0, ensuring consistent watering and support for tall stems.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Carrot','1. Preparation (2 weeks before planting): Prepare loose, sandy loam soil free of stones for optimal root development.\n2. Sowing (Spring or Fall): Sow seeds directly into prepared beds at a depth of about 1/4 inch, spacing them about an inch apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly to keep soil moist but not soggy; thin seedlings as they grow to prevent overcrowding.\n4. Harvesting (70-80 days after sowing): Harvest when roots are mature but still tender, pulling them from the ground carefully.','Loamy','To grow carrots, select varieties like Nantes, Imperator, or Danvers based on local climate and soil conditions. Carrots prefer cool temperatures between 55°F and 75°F and thrive in well-draining, loose soil with a pH of 6.0 to 7.0. Directly sow seeds in the garden, spacing them according to the variety, and maintain consistent moisture for proper germination. Incorporate well-rotted compost before planting, avoiding high-nitrogen fertilizers that can hinder root growth. Thin seedlings for adequate spacing, and harvest when they reach the desired size. Store harvested carrots in a cool, dark place or in the refrigerator for extended freshness.','Moderate','Rabi'),('Cauliflower','1. Preparation (4-6 weeks before planting): Prepare rich, well-drained soil with plenty of organic matter; choose a sunny location.\n2. Sowing (Indoors or Outdoors): Sow seeds indoors or directly outdoors in early spring or late summer for fall crops.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly and weed to prevent competition; apply nitrogen fertilizer as needed.\n4. Harvesting (60-100 days after sowing): Harvest when curds are firm and white, cutting at the base of the plant.','Loamy','To successfully grow cauliflower, select varieties like Snowball, Graffiti, or Cheddar that suit your climate. Cauliflower thrives in cool temperatures between 50°F and 75°F and is ideal for spring and fall planting. Use well-drained, fertile soil with a pH of 6.0 to 7.5, enriched with organic matter. Consistent moisture is crucial for head formation, so water regularly and apply mulch to retain moisture. Fertilize before planting and throughout the growing season as needed. Harvest when heads are compact and firm, using a sharp knife to cut the main head. Store in the refrigerator for up to a week or blanch and freeze for longer preservation.','Moderate','Rabi'),('Chickpea','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Test soil for pH and nutrients; prepare well-drained loamy soil with good aeration.\n2. Sowing (January-March): Sow seeds at a depth of 4-5 cm with spacing of about 30-50 cm between rows and 8-10 cm between plants.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water adequately but avoid waterlogging; monitor for pests and diseases, especially during flowering.\n4. Harvesting (July-August): Harvest when pods are dry; use manual or mechanical methods for efficient collection of seeds.','Red','Select chickpea varieties like Pusa 362, Pusa 372, or Pusa 405, suitable for Indian climates, and sow them during the rabi season (October-November) in well-drained loamy soil with a pH of 6.0-7.0. Ensure even land leveling and consider pre-soaking seeds to improve germination. Maintain consistent moisture during flowering and pod formation using furrow or drip irrigation, and apply compost before sowing, supplemented by necessary fertilizers. Harvest when pods turn yellow and dry them in the shade before threshing. Store seeds in moisture-free conditions and keep detailed records of inputs and yields, while exploring local market demand and value-added products for increased profitability.','Low','Rabi'),('Chillies','1. Preparation (6-8 weeks before planting): Start seeds indoors in seed trays with rich potting mix; ensure warm conditions for germination.\n2. Sowing/Transplanting (After last frost): Transplant seedlings outdoors when they are about 6-8 inches tall, spacing them about 18-24 inches apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water consistently and fertilize every few weeks; control pests as needed.\n4. Harvesting (70-90 days after transplanting): Harvest when fruits are fully colored and firm to touch.','Loamy','To cultivate chillies (Capsicum species), select a variety that matches your preferred heat level and culinary uses. They thrive in warm climates, requiring temperatures between 20°C to 30°C (68°F to 86°F) and plenty of sunlight (6-8 hours daily). Plant in well-draining, fertile soil with a pH of 6.0 to 7.0, enriched with organic matter. Start seeds indoors and transplant seedlings 18 to 24 inches apart. Maintain consistent moisture, allowing soil to dry between watering, and use balanced fertilizer, especially high in phosphorus and potassium during fruiting. Harvest when chillies reach the desired size and color, storing them in a cool, dry place or drying them for longer shelf life.','Moderate','Kharif'),('Chrysanthemum','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Choose a sunny location with well-drained soil; amend with compost or well-rotted manure.\n2. Sowing (Indoors February-March or Outdoors April-May): Sow seeds indoors or transplant rooted cuttings outdoors after frost danger has passed.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly and pinch back tips to encourage bushy growth; apply fertilizer every few weeks.\n4. Harvesting (Fall): Harvest flowers when fully open for best display in arrangements.','Loamy','To cultivate chrysanthemums effectively, select varieties suited to Indian climates, such as single-flowered, pompon, or decorative types, considering their size, shape, and color. Prepare well-drained loamy soil with a pH of 6.0-7.0, enhancing it with compost. Plant during the post-monsoon season (October-November) or early spring (February-March), spacing seedlings 12-18 inches apart. Ensure consistent moisture by watering at the base and applying mulch to suppress weeds. Fertilize in spring, provide support for taller varieties, and monitor for pests like aphids. Harvest flowers fully open for longevity, store in cool, dark places, and maintain records to improve future cultivation efforts.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Coriander Leaves','1. Preparation (2 weeks before planting): Prepare well-drained soil enriched with compost; choose a sunny location.\n                     2. Sowing (Spring or Fall): Sow seeds directly into prepared beds at a depth of about 1/4 inch, spacing them about 6 inches apart.\n                     3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly to keep soil moist but not soggy; thin seedlings as they grow.\n                     4. Harvesting (30-50 days after sowing): Harvest leaves when they are young and fresh; cut leaves above the ground level without uprooting the plant.','Loamy','To grow coriander leaves (Dhaniya), select suitable varieties like Cruiser or Calypso, considering your climate. Coriander thrives in cool to warm conditions, preferring temperatures between 50°F and 85°F (10°C to 29°C). Plant seeds in well-draining soil with a pH of 6.0 to 7.0, enhancing fertility with organic matter. Sow seeds in early spring or late summer, ensuring moderate moisture without waterlogging. Use organic mulch to retain moisture and suppress weeds. Harvest young leaves around 3-4 weeks after planting by cutting them with scissors. For the best flavor, use fresh coriander promptly, while storing seeds in a cool, dry place for future use.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Coriander Seeds','1. Preparation (2 weeks before planting): Prepare well-drained soil enriched with compost; choose a sunny location. \n2. Sowing (Spring or Fall): Sow seeds directly into prepared beds at a depth of about 1/4 inch, spacing them about 6 inches apart. \n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly to keep soil moist but not soggy; thin seedlings as they grow. \n4. Harvesting (60-90 days after sowing): Harvest when seeds turn brown and dry on the plant; cut stems and dry further if needed.','Loamy','To grow coriander (Coriandrum sativum), select varieties suited for seed production, such as Cruiser or Calypso. Coriander thrives in cool to warm climates, preferring temperatures between 50°F to 85°F (10°C to 30°C) and well-draining, fertile soil with a pH of 6.0 to 7.5. Directly sow seeds 1/4 to 1/2 inch deep, spaced 6 to 8 inches apart, from early spring to late fall. Maintain consistent moisture without overwatering, and apply organic mulch to retain moisture and suppress weeds. Harvest leaves when mature and cut seed heads when they turn brown, drying them in a ventilated area for storage.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Cotton','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Prepare fertile, well-drained soil by plowing and leveling; add organic matter if necessary. \n2. Sowing (April-May): Sow seeds at a depth of about 2-5 cm in rows spaced about 75 cm apart. \n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly during dry spells; monitor for pests like boll weevils and aphids. \n4. Harvesting (September-October): Harvest when cotton bolls open fully; use mechanical pickers or hand-picking methods.','Black','Select cotton varieties such as Bt Cotton (Bollgard and Bollgard II) or non-Bt varieties like MCU-5, H-4, and NHH-44 for Indian climates. Plant during the kharif season in well-drained loamy soil with a pH of 6.0-7.5, ensuring moisture during flowering and boll development.','Moderate to High','Kharif'),('Cumin','1. Preparation (2 weeks before planting): Prepare well-drained sandy loam soil enriched with organic matter. \n2. Sowing (Spring or Fall): Sow seeds directly into prepared beds at a depth of about 1/4 inch, spacing them about 6 inches apart. \n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly but avoid overwatering; control weeds through manual or chemical means. \n4. Harvesting (90-120 days after sowing): Harvest when seed heads turn brown and dry; cut plants and thresh to collect seeds.','Loamy','To grow cumin (Cuminum cyminum), select varieties like Indian or Syrian cumin suited to your climate. Cumin thrives in warm temperatures between 68°F to 86°F (20°C to 30°C) and prefers well-draining, loamy soil with a pH of 6.0 to 7.5. Sow seeds directly in the garden after the last frost, about 1/4 to 1/2 inch deep and 4 to 6 inches apart. While drought-tolerant, cumin needs moderate watering during its growing season. Incorporate compost before planting and apply mulch to retain moisture. Harvest seeds when heads turn brown, allowing them to dry before storing in airtight containers for maximum flavor retention.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Ginger','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Choose a sunny location with well-drained soil; prepare by adding compost. \n2. Sowing/Planting (Spring): Plant ginger rhizomes 2-4 inches deep, spacing them about 12 inches apart. \n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly to keep the soil moist; mulch to retain moisture and suppress weeds. \n4. Harvesting (8-10 months after planting): Harvest when leaves turn yellow and start dying back; gently dig up rhizomes.','Loamy','To grow ginger (Zingiber officinale), select a suitable variety such as Indian, Chinese, or Jamaican ginger. It thrives in tropical and subtropical climates with temperatures between 75°F to 85°F (24°C to 29°C) and high humidity. Plant healthy rhizomes in well-draining, loamy soil with a pH of 6.0 to 7.0, about 2 to 4 inches deep and spaced 8 to 12 inches apart. Water consistently to keep the soil moist, and use mulch to retain moisture and suppress weeds. Harvest ginger 8-10 months after planting, when the leaves turn yellow, and store cleaned rhizomes in a cool, dry place for long-term preservation.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Grape','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Choose a sunny location with well-drained soil; amend with compost if necessary.\n2. Sowing/Planting (Spring or Fall): Plant grapevines in prepared holes at a depth of about 6-8 inches, spacing them about 6-8 feet apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly but avoid overwatering; prune in late winter to promote growth and fruiting.\n4. Harvesting (August-September): Harvest when grapes are fully colored and sweet; cut clusters carefully from the vine.','Loamy','To grow grapes effectively, select varieties like Thompson Seedless, Concord, or Cabernet Sauvignon based on your climate and resistance to diseases. Plant in well-drained, loamy soil with a pH of 6.0 to 7.0, ideally in late winter or early spring. Space vines 6 to 10 feet apart and ensure they receive ample sunlight. Water regularly, allowing the soil to dry between watering, and use mulch to retain moisture. Support the vines with trellises and prune during dormancy to enhance air circulation and fruit production. Monitor for pests and diseases, harvest when grapes reach the desired sugar content, and store in a cool place for freshness.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Green Cardamom','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Select a shady location with well-drained, fertile soil; incorporate organic matter.\n2. Sowing/Planting (June-July): Plant healthy rhizomes or seeds 2-3 inches deep in pits spaced 3-5 feet apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly to maintain soil moisture; apply balanced fertilizers every few months.\n4. Harvesting (2-3 years after planting): Harvest pods when they turn light green to yellowish-green; pick by hand.','Loamy','To cultivate green cardamom (Elettaria cardamomum), select varieties suited to your climate and soil. This spice thrives in tropical regions with temperatures between 20°C and 35°C (68°F to 95°F) and prefers partial shade. Plant in well-draining, fertile soil with a pH of 6.0 to 8.0, enriched with organic matter. Propagate through seeds, spacing plants at least 3 feet apart. Provide consistent moisture, using mulch to retain soil humidity and suppress weeds. Apply balanced fertilizer before the growing season and during plant growth. Harvest pods when fully grown and green, storing them in a cool, dry place to preserve flavor.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Groundnut','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Prepare sandy loam soil with good drainage; add organic matter if necessary.\n2. Sowing (May-June): Sow seeds at a depth of about 5-8 cm in rows spaced about 30-45 cm apart, ensuring good soil contact with seeds.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly, especially during flowering and pod development; monitor for pests like leafcutter bees and aphids.\n4. Harvesting (September-October): Harvest when leaves turn yellow and pods are mature; dig up plants carefully to avoid damage to pods.','Sandy Loam','Choose groundnut varieties like TMV 2, TG 37A, and Kadiri 6, suitable for Indian climates, and plant during the kharif season in well-drained sandy loam soil with a pH of 6.0-7.0. Ensure moisture during flowering and pod formation using furrow or drip irrigation, and harvest when leaves yellow.','Moderate','Kharif'),('Guava','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Prepare well-drained soil enriched with organic matter; choose a sunny location.\n2. Sowing/Planting (June-July): Plant guava saplings or seedlings at a spacing of about 6m x 6m.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water every week during dry spells; fertilize twice a year based on soil tests.\n4. Harvesting (8 years after planting): Harvest when fruits are ripe and fragrant, usually in summer or fall.','Loamy','To cultivate guavas successfully, select varieties like Allahabad Safeda, Lucknow 49, or Pink Supreme based on your climate. Plant in a sunny location with well-drained loamy soil, ideally with a pH of 5.5 to 7.0. Guava trees can be planted year-round in tropical regions but should be planted in warmer months in cooler areas. Water consistently, especially during dry periods, and apply mulch to retain moisture. Use a balanced fertilizer with potassium and phosphorus, and prune regularly to improve air circulation. Harvest guavas when ripe, storing them at room temperature or refrigerating them for longer shelf life. Explore market opportunities for fresh and value-added products like jams.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Hemp','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Prepare well-drained soil with good nutrient levels; ensure a pH of 6-7. \n2. Sowing (April-May): Sow seeds at a depth of about 1-2 inches; space seeds 4-6 inches apart. \n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly, especially during dry spells; monitor for pests and diseases. \n4. Harvesting (August-September): Harvest when plants are between early bloom and seed set; cut stalks and allow them to undergo retting for fiber separation (4-6 weeks).','Loamy','Select low-THC hemp varieties suitable for Indian climates and plant during the kharif season in well-drained loamy soil with a pH of 6.0-7.0. Ensure consistent moisture during early growth and apply farmyard manure.','Moderate','Kharif'),('Jackfruit','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Choose a sunny location with well-drained soil; dig a hole of 1x1x1m filled with compost.\n2. Sowing/Planting (June-August): Plant seeds or grafted saplings; ensure spacing of 20-30 feet between plants.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly to keep the soil moist; apply balanced fertilizer (N:P:K 10-10-10) every 6-8 weeks after the second year.\n4. Harvesting (3-7 years after planting): Harvest when fruits are mature and color changes; handle carefully to avoid bruising.','Loamy','To grow jackfruits, select suitable varieties like Artocarpus heterophyllus or Cempedak based on your climate. Jackfruits thrive in tropical regions, requiring consistent warmth and well-distributed rainfall. They prefer well-drained, fertile soil with a pH of 6.0 to 7.5, ideally loamy and rich in organic matter. Plant seeds or saplings in prepared soil during warm seasons, ensuring ample spacing for growth. Maintain consistent moisture, particularly in dry spells, and use mulch to retain water and suppress weeds. Fertilize with a balanced mix during the growing season, monitor for pests, and harvest when the fruit emits a strong aroma and turns yellow.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Jasmine','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Select a sunny location with well-drained soil enriched with organic matter.\n2. Sowing/Planting (Spring or Fall): Plant jasmine cuttings or young plants in prepared holes spaced about 3 feet apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly but allow the soil to dry slightly between waterings; prune after flowering to maintain shape.\n4. Harvesting (Summer-Fall): Harvest flowers early in the morning when they are fully open for use in perfumes or teas.','Loamy','To grow jasmine effectively in India, select varieties like Jasminum sambac (Mogra) or Jasminum grandiflorum (Chameli), focusing on fragrance, flower size, and growth habit. Prepare well-drained loamy soil with a slightly acidic to neutral pH (6.0-7.0), incorporating organic matter. Plant jasmine in the post-monsoon season (October-November) or early spring (February-March), spacing seedlings or cuttings about 6-8 feet apart. Regular watering is essential, especially during dry spells, with mulching to retain moisture. Prune after flowering to control size, and harvest flowers in the morning for optimal fragrance. Keep records for continuous improvement and explore market demand for fresh flowers and related products.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Jowar','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Clear the field, plow, and harrow to create a fine seedbed; add farmyard manure.\n2. Sowing (June-July): Sow seeds in rows at a depth of 3-4 cm with spacing of 45-60 cm between rows and 15-20 cm between plants.\n3. Fertilization (At sowing and after 30 days): Fertilize with nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium; top-dress with nitrogen after 30 days.\n4. Harvesting (September-October): Harvest when grains are hard, cut close to the ground, and bundle stalks for threshing.','Black','Selecting a suitable jowar variety, such as Maldandi or Gujarat Jowar, is essential for Indian climates, especially for drought tolerance. Jowar grows well in loamy, slightly acidic soil (pH 6.0-7.5) and suits both kharif and rabi seasons, planted in June or July with monsoon rains. Leveling the field aids water distribution, while transplanting occurs at 25-30 days with a row spacing of 30-45 cm. Moderate irrigation, particularly during flowering, is vital. Regular weeding, pest monitoring, and proper fertilization support healthy growth. After mature grains are harvested and dried, store jowar in ventilated areas, and consider selling value-added products or joining cooperatives to increase profitability.','Moderate','Kharif'),('Jute','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Clear the land and prepare well-drained soil; ensure adequate moisture availability. \n2. Sowing (March-May): Sow seeds directly in the field at a depth of about 2-3 cm; thin seedlings when they reach 15-20 cm in height. \n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly; monitor for pests and diseases. \n4. Harvesting (July-August): Harvest when plants are mature (about 120 days after sowing); cut stalks close to the ground and soak in water for about 20 days for retting before extracting fibers.','Alluvial','Select jute varieties like Tossa Jute and White Jute suitable for Indian climates, and plant during the kharif season in well-drained loamy soil with a pH of 6.0-7.5. Consistent moisture is needed during early growth.','High','Kharif'),('Lily','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Select a sunny location with well-drained soil; amend soil with compost or peat moss.\n2. Sowing/Planting (Spring or Fall): Plant lily bulbs at a depth of about 6-8 inches, spacing them about 12 inches apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly but avoid waterlogging; fertilize every few weeks during active growth.\n4. Harvesting (Summer): Harvest blooms when they are just beginning to open for best vase life.','Loamy','To successfully grow lilies in India, select varieties such as Asiatic, Oriental, or hybrids based on color, size, and fragrance. Prepare well-drained loamy soil with a slightly acidic to neutral pH (6.0-7.0), enriching it with compost. Plant bulbs 6-8 inches deep and 8-12 inches apart during the post-monsoon season (October-November) or early spring (February-March). Ensure consistent moisture by watering at the base and applying mulch. Fertilize in spring, support tall varieties, and monitor for pests like aphids. Harvest flowers fully open for longevity, store them in cool, dark places, and maintain records to enhance future cultivation practices.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Litchi','1. Preparation (6 months before planting): Select a sunny location with well-drained loamy soil rich in organic matter.\n2. Sowing/Planting (Spring or Fall): Plant litchi seeds or young trees at a depth of about 5-10 cm, spacing them about 20 feet apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly during dry spells; fertilize twice yearly with balanced fertilizer.\n4. Harvesting (5-7 years after planting): Harvest when fruits turn bright red and are slightly soft to touch.','Loamy','To grow litchis, select varieties like Brewster, Mauritius, or Emperor based on your climate. Litchis thrive in subtropical to tropical regions, requiring winter chilling for optimal fruiting. Plant them in well-drained, sandy loam soil with a slightly acidic to neutral pH, enriched with organic matter. Propagate young trees from seeds or air-layering, spacing them adequately. Provide consistent moisture, especially during dry spells and fruit development, using mulch to retain moisture. Fertilize with a balanced mix in early spring, and monitor for pests and diseases. Harvest litchis when their skin turns from green to red, storing them in a cool place for short-term use.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Mango','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Prepare well-drained soil with a pH of 5.5 to 7.5; dig pits of 1x1x1m size filled with compost and soil.\n2. Sowing/Planting (June-July): Plant grafted saplings or seeds about 1/2 inch deep; space saplings 8 meters apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly, especially during dry spells; provide support for young trees if needed.\n4. Harvesting (April-May): Harvest when fruits are mature and color changes; handle carefully to avoid bruising.','Loamy','To grow mango trees successfully, select varieties like Alphonso, Kesar, or Dasheri, considering local climate and disease resistance. Plant in well-drained, sunny areas, ideally during late winter to early spring for optimal root establishment before the monsoon. Prepare the soil with organic matter, ensuring proper drainage, and maintain adequate spacing. Water regularly until established, as mango trees are drought-tolerant once mature. Fertilize in spring and summer with a balanced fertilizer, pruning for shape and air circulation. Monitor for pests and diseases, harvesting mangoes when ripe. Keep records of care practices and align harvest with market demand for increased profitability.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Marigold','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Choose a sunny spot with well-drained soil; prepare by loosening the soil and adding compost.\n2. Sowing (Indoors March-April or Outdoors May-June): Sow seeds indoors or directly outdoors after the last frost date.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly and deadhead spent blooms to encourage more flowers; fertilize monthly.\n4. Harvesting (Summer-Fall): Harvest flowers when fully open for use in arrangements or as companion plants.','Loamy','To successfully grow marigolds in India, select suitable varieties like African (Tagetes erecta) or French marigolds (Tagetes patula), considering flower size, color, and growth habit. Prepare well-drained loamy soil with a slightly acidic to neutral pH (6.0-7.0), enriching it with compost. Plant seeds or seedlings during the post-monsoon season (October-November) or early spring (February-March) at 8-12 inch spacing. While marigolds are drought-tolerant, regular watering is beneficial, especially during dry spells. Fertilize in spring and monitor for pests like aphids. Harvest flowers at full bloom in the morning, and maintain records for continuous improvement and profit strategies in local markets.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Masoor','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Prepare the soil by tilling and adding organic matter; ensure good drainage.\n2. Sowing (April-May): Sow seeds at a depth of about 3-5 cm with row spacing of about 30 cm apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water as needed; control weeds and monitor for diseases such as rust or blight.\n4. Harvesting (July-August): Harvest when the plants turn yellow and pods are dry; thresh to separate lentils from the pods.','Loamy','Select masoor varieties like Pusa Vaibhav, Pusa Ratna, or Pant L-407, suitable for Indian climates, and plant during the rabi season in well-drained loamy soil with a pH of 6.0-7.0. Even land leveling, pre-soaking seeds, and maintaining moisture during flowering and pod formation are essential.','Moderate','Rabi'),('Mint Leaves','1. Preparation (2 weeks before planting): Choose a location with well-drained soil and partial shade; prepare by adding compost.\n                2. Sowing/Transplanting (Spring or Fall): Plant mint cuttings or seeds in prepared beds at a depth of about 1 inch, spacing them about 12 inches apart.\n                3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly to keep soil moist but not waterlogged; trim plants to encourage bushy growth.\n                4. Harvesting (60-90 days after planting): Harvest leaves throughout the growing season by cutting stems above the ground level.','Loamy','To grow mint leaves, select a variety like peppermint or spearmint, considering your climate. Mint thrives in temperate to warm regions, preferring temperatures of 65°F to 75°F (18°C to 24°C). Plant mint in well-draining, fertile soil with a pH of 6.0 to 7.0. It is commonly propagated from cuttings or established plants, spaced appropriately to manage its rapid growth. Mint requires consistent moisture, so water regularly and use mulch to retain moisture and suppress weeds. Harvest leaves once plants reach 6-8 inches, cutting above healthy leaves to promote bushiness. Use fresh mint promptly or dry it for later use.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Moong','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Prepare well-drained sandy loam soil; ensure a pH of 6.2 to 7.2.\n2. Sowing (April-May): Sow seeds at a depth of 2-5 cm in rows spaced 30-36 inches apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly, especially during germination and flowering; monitor for pests like thrips.\n4. Harvesting (July-August): Harvest when pods are mature (about 60% darkened); dry the seeds completely before storage.','Loamy','Select moong varieties like Pusa Vishal, Pusa Ratna, or PDM-139 that suit Indian climates. Sow during the kharif season (June-July) in loamy soil with slightly acidic to neutral pH, leveraging monsoon rains. Provide consistent moisture using drip or furrow irrigation during critical growth stages. Apply compost before planting and supplement with essential nutrients. Harvest when pods are green and developed, and dry in shaded areas before threshing. Store seeds in a cool, pest-free environment. Track market demand and explore options for split moong to increase profitability. Record crop data and use crop rotation to improve soil health and productivity.','Moderate','Kharif'),('Mustard','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Prepare the land by plowing and leveling; incorporate organic matter into the soil. \n2. Sowing (October-November): Sow seeds at a depth of about 1-2 cm in rows spaced about 30 cm apart. \n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water as needed, especially during flowering; monitor for pests like aphids. \n4. Harvesting (March-April): Harvest when seed pods turn brown and dry; thresh to separate seeds from pods.','Loamy','Select suitable mustard varieties like RGN-26, Varuna, or Pusa Jaikisan for Indian climates, and plant during the rabi season in well-drained loamy soil with a pH of 6.0-7.0. Ensure moisture during flowering and apply farmyard manure and fertilizers.','Moderate','Rabi'),('Nutmeg','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Choose a tropical location with well-drained soil rich in organic matter. \n2. Sowing/Planting (Spring or Summer): Plant nutmeg seeds or seedlings in holes spaced about 20 feet apart. \n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly and apply mulch to retain moisture; fertilize every few months. \n4. Harvesting (6-8 years after planting): Harvest when fruits are ripe and split open, revealing the nutmeg seed inside.','Loamy','To grow nutmeg (Myristica fragrans), select varieties suited for tropical or subtropical climates. Nutmeg thrives in temperatures between 77°F to 95°F (25°C to 35°C) and prefers well-draining, fertile soil with a pH of 6.0 to 7.5, rich in organic matter. Propagate by planting fresh seeds 1 to 2 inches deep, ensuring adequate spacing as trees can reach heights of 30 to 40 feet. Regular watering is essential, but avoid waterlogging. Apply balanced fertilizers during the growing season and mulch to conserve moisture. Nutmeg trees typically begin fruiting after 7-9 years, yielding both nutmeg and mace upon harvesting. Store dried seeds in airtight containers.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Onion','1. Preparation (6-8 weeks before planting): Prepare well-drained soil enriched with organic matter; choose a sunny location.\n2. Sowing/Planting (Spring or Fall): Plant onion sets or seeds about 1 inch deep and spaced about 4 inches apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly but avoid waterlogging; apply nitrogen fertilizer as needed during growth stages.\n4. Harvesting (90-120 days after planting): Harvest when tops begin to yellow and fall over; cure onions in a dry place before storage.','Loamy','To grow onions effectively, select suitable varieties such as Yellow, Red, or White based on your region and intended use. Onions thrive in cool to temperate climates and can tolerate frost, making them ideal for both spring and fall planting. Use well-drained, fertile soil with a pH of 6.0 to 7.5, enriching it with organic matter like compost. Plant seeds, sets, or transplants, ensuring consistent moisture, particularly during bulb formation. Mulch to retain moisture and suppress weeds. Harvest when tops fall over, cure the bulbs in a shaded area, and store them in a cool, dry place to prolong freshness.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Orange','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Choose a sunny location with well-drained soil; amend with compost if necessary. \n2. Sowing/Planting (Spring or Fall): Plant orange saplings at the same depth they were grown in their nursery containers, spacing them about 12-20 feet apart. \n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly during dry spells and apply citrus fertilizer every few months. \n4. Harvesting (8-12 months after flowering): Harvest when oranges are fully colored and slightly soft to touch.','Loamy','To grow oranges (Citrus sinensis), select a variety suitable for your region, such as Valencia, Navel, or Blood Orange. Oranges thrive in subtropical to tropical climates with temperatures between 55°F to 100°F (13°C to 38°C) and prefer well-draining sandy-loam soil with a pH of 6.0 to 7.5. Typically grown from grafted trees, plant them in full sunlight with adequate spacing. Regular watering is crucial, especially during dry periods, and mulching helps retain moisture. Fertilize in late winter or early spring and monitor for pests and diseases. Harvest when oranges are ripe, storing them in a cool, dark place for freshness.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Paddy Rice','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Prepare the land by tilling and flooding the field with 2-2.5 cm of water.\n2. Sowing (April-May): Transplant seedlings (40 days old) or directly sow seeds.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Maintain water levels and monitor for pests/diseases.\n4. Harvesting (September-October): Harvest during dry weather, threshing to separate grains from husks.','Alluvial','Selecting rice varieties like Basmati, Sona Masuri, IR-64, or Swarna is crucial for Indian climates. Basmati is particularly prized for its aromatic long grain. Rice is typically planted in June or July with the monsoon season, on flat land with good water retention. Transplant seedlings at 25-30 days, spacing them 15x15 cm to 20x20 cm. Proper water management, consistent fertilization, and vigilant pest control help maintain crop health. Once mature, rice is harvested, dried, and threshed. Store in a cool, dry area to prevent spoilage. Timing the sale with high-demand seasons and participating in cooperatives can increase profitability.','High','Kharif'),('Papaya','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Choose a sunny location with well-drained soil; amend with organic matter as needed.\n2. Sowing (Year-round in warm climates): Sow seeds directly in the ground or start indoors; plant seeds about 1/2 inch deep, spacing them about 3 feet apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly and fertilize every few weeks with a balanced fertilizer.\n4. Harvesting (6-12 months after planting): Harvest when fruits turn yellow and are slightly soft to touch.','Loamy','To grow papayas successfully, select suitable varieties like Red Lady, Solo, or Sunrise based on your climate. Choose a sunny site with well-drained sandy-loam soil, ideally with a pH of 6.0 to 6.5. Plant seeds at a depth of 1 inch or transplant seedlings while ensuring consistent moisture, especially during fruit development. Fertilize with a balanced mix, emphasizing potassium, and avoid excessive nitrogen. Prune dead or diseased growth and monitor for pests like aphids. Harvest when the skin turns yellow or orange, and store at room temperature until ripe. Build relationships with local vendors for market access and consider value-added products for extra income.','High','Zaid'),('Pineapple','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Choose a sunny location with well-drained sandy loam soil; amend as needed for fertility.\n2. Sowing/Planting (Spring or Summer): Plant pineapple crowns or slips at a depth of about 2 inches, spacing them about 3 feet apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly but allow the soil to dry out between watering; fertilize every few months with balanced fertilizer.\n4. Harvesting (18-24 months after planting): Harvest when the fruit is fully developed and turns golden-yellow.','Loamy','To grow pineapples, select varieties like Smooth Cayenne, Queen Victoria, or MD2 suited to your climate. Pineapples thrive in tropical regions with warm temperatures and require well-drained, sandy loam soil with a slightly acidic to neutral pH. They are typically planted from the crowns of mature fruits; allow the crown to dry before planting. Maintain consistent moisture while avoiding overwatering to prevent root rot. Fertilize regularly with balanced nutrients and apply organic mulch to retain moisture and suppress weeds. Harvest when the fruit is ripe, and store at room temperature or refrigerate for longer freshness. Regularly prune dead leaves to promote health.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Pomegranate','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Prepare sandy loam soil with good drainage; amend with compost if necessary.\n2. Sowing/Planting (Spring or Fall): Plant pomegranate seeds or seedlings at a depth of about 5-7 cm, spacing them about 12-15 feet apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly but allow the soil to dry between watering; provide support for young plants if necessary.\n4. Harvesting (5-7 months after flowering): Harvest when fruits are fully colored and sound hollow when tapped.','Loamy','To successfully grow pomegranates, select suitable varieties like Wonderful, Ganesh, or Mridula based on your climate. Plant in a sunny area with well-drained loamy soil, maintaining a pH of 5.5 to 7.0. Plant during mild winters or late winter to early spring in warmer climates, avoiding heavy rainfall periods. Water regularly, ensuring the soil dries between watering, and apply mulch for moisture retention. Use a balanced fertilizer enriched with potassium and phosphorus, and prune for shape and air circulation. Harvest when pomegranates exhibit full color and a metallic sound. Store at room temperature or refrigerate, exploring market opportunities for fresh or processed products.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Potato','1. Preparation (2-4 weeks before planting): Choose a sunny location; prepare soil by digging trenches about 6 inches deep and adding compost.\n2. Sowing (Spring): Plant seed potatoes with eyes facing up, spacing them about 12 inches apart in rows.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly; hill soil around plants as they grow to encourage tuber formation.\n4. Harvesting (60-120 days after planting): Harvest when foliage dies back; gently loosen the soil and collect tubers.','Loamy','To grow potatoes, select varieties like Russet, Yukon Gold, or Red Pontiac based on climate and intended use. Potatoes thrive in cool to temperate regions, requiring well-drained, loamy soil with a pH of 5.8 to 6.5. Plant seed potatoes in early spring or late summer, about 4-6 inches deep and spaced 12-15 inches apart. Consistent moisture is crucial, especially during tuber formation, so water deeply and use mulch to retain moisture. Fertilize before planting and monitor for pests like Colorado potato beetles. Harvest when plants flower and tops begin to die back, curing potatoes before storing in a cool, dark place.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Ragi','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Prepare the land by tilling and leveling; incorporate organic matter or fertilizers into the soil.\n2. Sowing (June-July): Sow seeds at a depth of about 2-3 cm in rows spaced approximately 25-30 cm apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Ensure adequate moisture during germination; weed regularly to prevent competition for nutrients.\n4. Harvesting (September-October): Harvest when grains are hard; use manual methods or machinery for efficient harvesting and threshing.','Red','Select drought-resistant ragi varieties like GPU-28, Indaf-5, and VL-145 for optimal growth in Indias arid regions. Ragi thrives in well-drained, sandy-loam soil and is typically sown during the kharif season (June-July), although rabi planting is also possible. Sow seeds directly at a depth of 2-3 cm, spaced 30-45 cm apart. While ragi is drought-tolerant, it benefits from irrigation during key growth stages. Apply farmyard manure and necessary fertilizers. Harvest when grains turn golden brown, dry in the field, and store in a cool, dry place. Market ragi or value-added products like flour for better profits.','Moderate','Kharif'),('Rose','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Choose a sunny location with well-drained soil; amend soil with compost.\n2. Sowing/Planting (Spring or Fall): Plant bare-root roses or seedlings in prepared holes; ensure proper spacing of about 2-3 feet apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly, especially during dry spells; prune in late winter or early spring to promote growth and flowering.\n4. Harvesting (Summer-Fall): Cut flowers when buds are just starting to open for best freshness and longevity in arrangements.','Loamy','Select suitable rose varieties like hybrid tea, floribunda, and climbing roses for Indian climates. Plant during the post-monsoon or spring season in well-drained loamy soil with a pH of 6.0-7.0, ensuring deep watering and regular fertilization.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Soybean','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Test soil for nutrients; prepare well-drained soil rich in organic matter. \n2. Sowing (May-June): Sow seeds at a depth of about 2-5 cm in rows spaced about 30-45 cm apart. \n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly, especially during flowering and pod filling stages; monitor for pests like aphids and soybean loopers. \n4. Harvesting (September-October): Harvest when leaves yellow and pods are firm; use combines or manual methods for collection.','Loamy','Select soybean varieties like JS 335, JS 9560, and NRC 37 for Indian climates, and plant during the kharif season in well-drained loamy soil with a pH of 6.0-7.0. Ensure consistent moisture during flowering and pod formation and harvest when 80-90% of pods turn brown.','Moderate','Kharif'),('Sunflower','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Prepare well-drained soil with good fertility; incorporate compost or fertilizers as needed. \n2. Sowing (April-May): Sow seeds at a depth of about 2-5 cm in rows spaced about 70-90 cm apart. \n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly but avoid overwatering; control weeds through cultivation or herbicides. \n4. Harvesting (August-September): Harvest when back of heads turn yellow and seeds are firm; use combines for efficient harvesting.','Loamy','Choose sunflower varieties like PAC 36, KBSH-41, or MSFH-17 for Indian climates, and plant during the kharif season in well-drained loamy soil with a pH of 6.0-7.0. Maintain moisture during flowering using furrow or drip irrigation.','Moderate','Kharif'),('Tomato','1. Preparation (6-8 weeks before planting): Start seeds indoors in seed trays with good potting mix; ensure warm conditions for germination.\n2. Sowing/Transplanting (After last frost): Transplant seedlings outdoors when they are about 6-8 inches tall, spacing them about 18-24 inches apart in rows.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water consistently; stake or cage plants for support as they grow; apply balanced fertilizer every few weeks.\n4. Harvesting (70-100 days after transplanting): Harvest when fruits are fully colored and firm to touch.','Loamy','To grow tomatoes successfully, select varieties like Roma, Beefsteak, or Cherry based on your climate and disease resistance. Tomatoes thrive in warm, sunny environments, so plant them after the last frost. Use well-drained, fertile soil with a pH of 6.0 to 7.0, amending it with compost for improved fertility. Water deeply and consistently, especially during fruit development, and apply organic mulch to retain moisture. Support plants with stakes or cages, and prune to enhance air circulation. Harvest when tomatoes are fully colored and firm, storing them at room temperature until ripe and refrigerating only when necessary to extend shelf life.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Tuberose','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Prepare well-drained soil enriched with organic matter; choose a sunny location.\n2. Sowing/Planting (Spring): Plant tuberose bulbs at a depth of about 4-6 inches, spacing them about 12 inches apart.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly but avoid waterlogging; fertilize every few weeks with a balanced fertilizer.\n4. Harvesting (Late Summer-Fall): Harvest spikes when the first few flowers open; cut stems early in the morning for best quality.','Loamy','Select tuberose varieties like Polianthes tuberosa for Indian climates, and plant during the post-monsoon or early spring season in well-drained loamy soil with a pH of 6.0-7.0. Ensure consistent soil moisture and support for tall stems.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Turmeric','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Prepare well-drained soil enriched with organic matter; choose a sunny location. \n2. Sowing/Planting (Spring): Plant turmeric rhizomes 2-4 inches deep, spacing them about 12 inches apart. \n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water regularly but avoid waterlogging; apply fertilizer every few months. \n4. Harvesting (7-10 months after planting): Harvest when leaves yellow and die back; dig up rhizomes carefully.','Loamy','To grow turmeric (Curcuma longa), select a suitable variety like Alleppey Supreme or Erode, and plant healthy rhizomes in spring or early summer. Turmeric thrives in tropical and subtropical climates, requiring temperatures between 77°F to 86°F (25°C to 30°C) and high humidity. Use well-draining, loamy soil with a pH of 6.0 to 7.5, ensuring good drainage to prevent rhizome rot. Water regularly to keep the soil moist but not waterlogged, and apply organic mulch to conserve moisture. Harvest turmeric 7-10 months after planting when leaves yellow, and store cleaned rhizomes in a cool, dry place for long-term preservation.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Urad','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Prepare the land by plowing and leveling; incorporate organic matter.\n2. Sowing (June-July): Sow seeds at a depth of 3-5 cm with spacing of 30-45 cm between rows.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Ensure adequate moisture; weed regularly and monitor for diseases and pests.\n4. Harvesting (September-October): Harvest when pods are dry; thresh to separate seeds from pods.','Loamy','Select suitable urad varieties like T9, Pant U-19, or UPU-2 for Indian climates, ideally sowing during the kharif season (June-July) in loamy soil with a pH of 6.0-7.0. Ensure proper land leveling to promote water distribution and use pre-soaked, high-quality seeds. Maintain consistent moisture through drip or furrow irrigation and apply compost before sowing, supplementing with necessary nutrients. Harvest when pods are green and dry them in the shade before threshing. Store seeds in well-ventilated areas to avoid pests. Keep records of inputs and yields while exploring local market demand and value-added products like split urad for enhanced profitability.','Moderate','Kharif'),('Watermelon','1. Preparation (2 weeks before planting): Choose a sunny location with well-drained soil; incorporate organic matter into the soil.\n2. Sowing (April-May): Sow seeds 1-2 inches deep in mounds spaced about 4 feet apart; thin to 2 plants per mound after emergence.\n3. Maintenance (Throughout growing season): Water deeply but infrequently; side-dress with nitrogen fertilizer as vines develop.\n4. Harvesting (July-August): Harvest when the tendril closest to the fruit turns brown and the bottom of the fruit turns yellow.','Loamy','To cultivate watermelons effectively, select varieties like Crimson Sweet, Sugar Baby, or Charleston Gray, considering your climate and disease resistance. Plant in a sunny area with well-drained sandy loam soil, ensuring a pH of 6.0 to 7.0. Seeds should be sown after the last frost, 1 to 2 inches deep, allowing ample space for sprawling vines. Maintain consistent moisture during flowering and fruiting, using drip irrigation to minimize disease risk. Fertilize with a balanced mix and prune for air circulation and fruit development. Harvest when tendrils turn brown, storing in cool, dry conditions. Keep records of practices and market demand for profitability.','Moderate','Zaid'),('Wheat','1. Preparation (1 month before planting): Prepare well-drained loamy soil by plowing and leveling; add fertilizers based on soil tests.\n2. Sowing (November-December): Sow seeds at a depth of about 5-7 cm in rows spaced about 20-25 cm apart.\n3. Irrigation (Throughout growing season): Irrigate as needed, especially during flowering and grain filling stages.\n4. Harvesting (April-May): Harvest when grains are golden brown; use sickles or harvesters followed by threshing.','Loamy','Select wheat varieties like HD 2967, PBW 343, and GW 366 that are well-suited for Indian climates, focusing on disease resistance and regional adaptability. Wheat grows best in well-drained loamy soil and is traditionally sown during the rabi season (October-December). Maintain consistent moisture through efficient irrigation, especially during critical growth stages, and apply farmyard manure along with nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium as needed. Harvest when the crop turns golden brown, drying it in the field before threshing. Store grains in a moisture-free, ventilated space. Explore market demand or government programs for selling, and consider wheat flour as a value-added product for greater profit.','Moderate','Rabi');
